<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tippecanoe River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tippecanoe River is a river of northern Indiana, flowing nearly 300 kilometres south-west to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wabash-river">Wabash River</a> above Lafayette. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a> stood near the confluence, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tippecanoe">Battle of Tippecanoe</a> in 1811 took its name from the river. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a>'s fame from the battle gave his 1840 presidential campaign the slogan 'Tippecanoe and Tyler Too'.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tippecanoe-river.jpg" alt="Tippecanoe River" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kmusser · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tippecanoerivermap.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Tippecanoe is a gentle river of 182 miles (293 km) in the Central Corn Belt Plains ecoregion. It rises in kettle lakes left by ancient glaciers in Noble County, flowing from Crooked Lake through Wilmot Pond, Backwaters Lake and Tippecanoe Lake in Kosciusko County. It then runs south and west through Marshall County, north-west through Fulton and Pulaski Counties, where it crosses Tippecanoe River State Park, and on through White and Carroll Counties into Tippecanoe County, where it joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wabash-river">Wabash River</a> near Battle Ground.</p><p>Eighty-eight natural lakes feed the river, and its drainage area of about 5,100 square kilometres spans 14 counties. Its name comes from a word of the Miami–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a> language for the buffalo fish. Besides Tippecanoe County, five townships along the river, in Carroll, Kosciusko, Marshall, Pulaski and Tippecanoe Counties, carry its name.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tippecanoe">Battle of Tippecanoe</a>, in which Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a> defeated the confederacy led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>, was fought near the south bank of the river, and modern Lafayette grew up near the site. Numerous dams were built along its course to create lakes and ponds for water power. In 1925 two hydroelectric plants were added: the Oakdale plant created Lake Freeman, reaching 7 miles north through Monticello, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> plant created Lake Shafer, reaching 6 miles north from Norway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river supports more imperilled species and a greater diversity of species than most streams of the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/midwestern-united-states">Midwest</a>, and the Nature Conservancy has named it one of the ten rivers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> most worth preserving. Surveys since 1903 have recorded 57 species of freshwater mussels, among them the northern riffleshell, the kidneyshell and the clubshell, which is federally endangered. Silt from dredging and changes in land use threaten them. Fish surveys of the early 21st century found 54 species in 14 families, nearly 80 per cent of them game fish, with bluegill the most common.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tippecanoe River” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1808 he and his brother Tecumseh founded Prophetstown near the junction of the <b>Tippecanoe</b> and Wabash rivers, drawing followers from many nations.</blockquote><a href="/state/tecumsehs-confederacy">Tecumseh's Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
